# OneTee local runner credentials.
# Copy this file to "local.env" (same folder) and fill in all three values.
#
# NOTHING REAL GOES IN THIS FILE. It is committed, the repo is public, and the
# three values below are the complete set of things needed to write to OneTee's
# database. The token alone is the credential, but publishing the account and
# database ids alongside it means a token that leaks anywhere else is instantly
# usable — so all three stay out of git. Your filled-in copy is local.env,
# which .gitignore keeps untracked.
#
# Where to find each one:
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID  dashboard -> Workers & Pages -> right sidebar,
#                          "Account ID"
#   CLOUDFLARE_D1_DB_ID    dashboard -> Storage & Databases -> D1 ->
#                          onetee-teetimes -> "Database ID"
#   CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN   dashboard -> My Profile -> API Tokens -> Create
#                          Token -> "Edit D1" template (or custom:
#                          Account | D1 | Edit). Same token as the
#                          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN repo secret, or a fresh one.
#
# All three are also GitHub repo secrets, under Settings -> Secrets and
# variables -> Actions, which is where the workflows read them from.

CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=your-cloudflare-account-id
CLOUDFLARE_D1_DB_ID=your-d1-database-id
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=your-d1-scoped-api-token
